WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:02.449
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:02.449 --> 00:00:07.010
The world is full of hidden wonders and sights
that will blow your mind.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:17.470
Let's go on a journey to some of the weirdest
places on earth.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:25.300
We start in Derweze, Turkmenistan where a
huge fiery crater is known to locals as "The

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.519
Door to Hell".

00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:34.160
Whilst drilling at the site in 1971, soviet
geologists tapped into an underground cavern

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.800
filled with natural gas.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:43.800
But the ground beneath the drilling rig collapsed,
leaving a hole 70 metres in diameter.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:50.010
Fearing the cavern would leak poisonous gasses
the drilling team decided to burn it off.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:56.609
They hoped it would burn out in a few days,
but it's still burning today, over 40 years

00:00:56.609 --> 00:00:57.609
later.

00:00:57.609 --> 00:01:03.429
Next to the US, specifically Yellowstone National
park in Wyoming.

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:11.470
Here you will find the world's third largest
hot spring, and one of the most beautiful.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:18.110
Named Grand Prismatic Spring, this springs
vibrant colours are caused by its pigmented

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:19.110
bacteria.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:25.090
Seasonal temperatures as well as levels of
chlorophyll and carotenoids in the spring,

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:31.600
greatly influence the tint of its bacteria,
and subsequently its colours.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.760
Bacteria doesn't form in the centre because
the water is too hot to sustain bacterial

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:37.760
life.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:44.830
Salar de Uyuni in Bolivia is home to the world's
largest salt flat, occupying the area of roughly

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:48.750
10,582 square kilometres (6,575 sq mi).

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:54.920
It was created when a prehistoric lake dried
up and left a salty crust behind.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:02:01.000
When it rains the entire salt flat becomes
a giant mirror, and you can walk on top of

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.650
a near perfect reflection of the sky.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:12.560
In South America amongst the Pakaraima's mountain
range sits the tabletop mountain Mount Roraima.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:20.620
It's one of the oldest geological formations
on Earth, dating back over two billion years.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:26.360
It serves as a triple border between Brazil,
Venezuela and Guyana.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:32.680
Its 400 metre tall cliffs have meant it's
been cut off from human interaction for most

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.900
of history.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:40.790
Subsequently the mountain's upper plateau
has it's own unique ecosystem and a staggering

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:46.750
30% of all the plant and animal species are
endemic to the mountain.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:52.970
Which means they can only be found on this
mountain and nowhere else on earth.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:59.040
But Mount Roraima isn't the only place on
earth with an isolated ecosystem, such places

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:06.990
are countless, but one of the most interesting
is Socotra in Yemen which is a small archipelago

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.950
in the Indian Ocean.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:15.170
Because it's so isolated, one third of its
plant life is found nowhere else on the planet,

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:21.230
such as the Dragon's Blood Tree, named after
its blood red sap.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.350
Have you ever wanted to step inside a glacier?

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:29.550
In Skaftafell, Iceland there is an ice cave
within glacier ice.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:35.270
This unique cave is a result of a glacial
mill, which is a hole in the glacier.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:41.290
Water flows down the hole and eventually melts
the ice to form a large cave.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:47.170
Because glaciers are constantly moving, if
you stand inside the cave you can hear incredibly

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:53.599
loud cracking sounds as the ice above you,
and the cave itself moves millimetres along

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:55.099
the ground.

00:03:55.099 --> 00:04:01.290
From one icy hemisphere to another, in the
Antarctic you'll find frozen waves.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:05.580
But these waves are a result of melting, not
freezing.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:09.320
The downwards pointing spikes are simply icicles.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:17.590
Next to China for the world's most colourful
mountain range, the Zhangye Danxia landform

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:18.969
in Gansu.

00:04:18.969 --> 00:04:24.909
This magnificent display of colours is the
result of red sandstone and mineral deposits

00:04:24.909 --> 00:04:29.130
being laid down for over 24 million years.

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:31.330
Fancy a spot of scuba diving?

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:38.189
The imaginatively named, Great Blue Hole is
a popular submarine sinkhole and scuba diving

00:04:38.189 --> 00:04:41.469
site just off the coast of Belize.

00:04:41.469 --> 00:04:46.580
This natural phenomenon is 124 metres (407
ft) deep.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:51.550
On Vaadhoo island in the Maldives is the Sea
of Stars.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:55.629
Beautiful bioluminescent patterns can be seen
along the shore.

00:04:55.629 --> 00:04:59.590
These lights are caused by marine microbes
called phytoplankton.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:07.069
They contain a chemical called luciferase
which has the ability to create biological

00:05:07.069 --> 00:05:08.069
light.

00:05:08.069 --> 00:05:14.199
If you're feeling romantic you may want to
visit the Tunnel of Love in Klevan, Ukraine.

00:05:14.199 --> 00:05:20.909
This hot spot for lovers is an active railway
line engulfed in a tunnel of leaves.

00:05:20.909 --> 00:05:26.910
The tunnel was formed over many years as a
train which passed through the forest 3 times

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:30.669
a day slowly carved a tunnel through the trees.

00:05:30.669 --> 00:05:37.969
But natural wonders also come in the manmade
variety, such as Fly Geyser in Nevada.

00:05:37.969 --> 00:05:45.970
Located on the private Fly Ranch and cut off
from tourists, this geyser is a man-made phenomenon,

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:52.949
created by well drilling in 1964 whilst seeking
sources of geothermal energy.

00:05:52.949 --> 00:05:59.419
The well was not capped correctly and dissolved
minerals started rising and accumulating.

00:05:59.419 --> 00:06:06.759
The mound beneath the geyser is still growing
today and water is constantly being released.

00:06:06.759 --> 00:06:12.319
Also man made is the hidden beach in the Marieta
Islands, Mexico.

00:06:12.319 --> 00:06:17.469
This beautiful secluded beach is actually
the result of bombing.

00:06:17.469 --> 00:06:22.539
The spot was bombed during target practice
by the Mexican government.

00:06:22.539 --> 00:06:28.849
Chand Baori is a famous step well in the Indian
state of Rajasthan.

00:06:28.849 --> 00:06:31.819
It was constructed in 800 CE.

00:06:31.819 --> 00:06:37.540
Rajasthan state is extremely arid and the
intent of the step well was to conserve as

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:41.199
much water as possible during the hotter months.

00:06:41.199 --> 00:06:47.629
The air constantly remains 5-6 degrees cooler
at the bottom, compared to the surface.

00:06:47.629 --> 00:06:53.719
Therefore it was commonly used as a community
gathering place for locals during periods

00:06:53.719 --> 00:06:57.699
of intense heat.

